    "guidePath": "/guides/is-refinancing-worth-it/",
    "notes": [
      "Simple recovery = closing costs / monthly payment reduction, only if the reduction is positive. It is a payment-based screen, including for financed fees; it is not a balance-aware economic break-even or a date when upfront cash was necessarily repaid.",
      "Five-year borrowing-cost saving = current interest through month 60 − new interest through month 60 − closing costs once. Derive this by reconciling payments and remaining balances at the same date. This excludes opportunity returns, taxes and any benefit from using cash-out proceeds.",
      "The $25,000 cash-out row supplies additional cash and finances the stated costs. Its one point is charged on current balance plus cash out, before adding financed fees. Do not call cash out a saving or treat this row as equal-proceeds financing.",
      "The fifteen-year case raises the monthly payment, so the tool has no simple payment-recovery number. A longer term can lower the payment while increasing lifetime interest. Old payments and fees already paid are sunk historical amounts.",
      "The original-loan task derives a balance from a regular original schedule; it cannot reconstruct prior extra payments, modifications or late postings. Use the actual current balance/payment when available. Check an actual Loan Estimate; no quotes or approval are supplied."
    ],
    "chartMetric": "Five-year interest-plus-fee savings",
    "chartTitle": "Refinance: five-year interest-plus-fee savings (USD)",
    "chartAlt": "Five-year borrowing-cost savings compare current interest with new interest and fees at month sixty, excluding the use of cash-out proceeds.",
    "chartCaption": "Matched month-60 borrowing costs, not a lifetime, equal-cash or investment-benefit ranking.",
    "checkedOn": "2026-11-19",
    "csvPath": "/data/reviewed-scenarios/refinance-20261119.csv",
    "chartPath": "/images/guides/reviewed-refinance-20261119.svg"
  },
