    "guidePath": "/guides/rent-vs-buy-a-house/",
    "notes": [
      "The calculator always projects 30 years. Year 10 is a checkpoint in that same schedule, not a new holding-period input. Buyer equity at each checkpoint assumes the entered selling cost and deducts the then-outstanding mortgage.",
      "The renter’s starting account is down payment plus purchase costs avoided, less rental deposit and upfront rental costs. Monthly ownership-minus-rental cash-flow differences are invested or withdrawn at month end; the refundable deposit is added to reported renter wealth.",
      "First annual crossing means the first year buyer equity is at least renter wealth. It is not an exact break-even month, a permanent lead or a probability of success. Not within 30 years means no crossing was found, not zero years.",
      "A negative renter account or terminal figure indicates an unfunded shortfall in this model, not a feasible investment portfolio or a financing offer. Additional borrowing costs to fund that shortfall are not simulated. Inspect cash resources as well as the signed wealth comparison.",
      "All returns, rents, growth and selling percentages are hypothetical. Mortgage insurance, utilities, unexpected repair timing, portfolio fees/taxes and personal income-tax benefits are outside these cases. The result is not a market forecast or a recommendation."
